From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz M <lukasz1992m+openwrt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: wed: do not start the WDMA without its rx rings

airoha_wed_start() ignores the return value of wed_wdma_rx_ring_setup()
and goes on to wed_hw_init() and wed_dma_enable(), which sets
WDMA_GLO_CFG_RX_DMA_EN.  A ring whose allocation failed has no
descriptor and its BASE/COUNT registers were not written by that call:
they keep their reset value on the first start, or the address of the
ring freed by an earlier detach on a restart.  The WDMA then reads
descriptors from PA 0 or from memory that is no longer a ring and writes
its completion state there.

The mainline mtk_wed_start() stops at this point and leaves the engines
off.  Do the same: the reset path already expects airoha_wed_start() to
leave everything off when it cannot restore the rings, so the Wi-Fi
driver keeps running with the offload disabled instead of handing the
WDMA an unfinished ring.

On the XX530v V2 both WDMA RX rings are set up by the TX ring setup of
the two bands before the start, so this is the single-band or
failed-allocation path rather than the one this board takes today.

Signed-off-by: Lukasz M <lukasz1992m+openwrt@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_wed.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_wed.c
@@ -881,9 +881,17 @@ static void airoha_wed_start(struct mtk_
 	if (dev->hw->reset_failed)
 		return;
 
-	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(dev->rx_wdma); i++)
-		if (!dev->rx_wdma[i].desc)
-			wed_wdma_rx_ring_setup(dev, i, 16, false);
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(dev->rx_wdma); i++) {
+		if (dev->rx_wdma[i].desc)
+			continue;
+
+		if (wed_wdma_rx_ring_setup(dev, i, 16, false)) {
+			dev_err(dev->hw->dev,
+				"wdma rx ring %d setup failed, offload stays off\n",
+				i);
+			return;
+		}
+	}
 
 	wed_hw_init(dev);
 	wed_configure_irq(dev, irq_mask);
